From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:10:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 444/453] fix(desktop): prove ownership of launcher-run remote
 backends (macOS) [daveey]
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Remote backends on a Mac host were never reused or reaped: W0's new
`reuse miss` log showed `pid … is not our dashboard` for the mini's own
backend. There `hermes` is a launcher that runs the backend as
`python -I -c "...; sys.argv = [... 'serve', '--isolated', ...,
'--ssh-owner-nonce', nonce]; ..."`, so the real argv exists only inside
the -c script. The ownership probe also read macOS argv by re-splitting
`ps -o command=` output, which breaks on the launcher's quoted script and
on paths like "Hermes Python.app".

- Read exact argv: /proc on Linux, sysctl KERN_PROCARGS2 on macOS, `ps`
  only as the last resort.
- When the OS argv has no `serve` and the process is `python ... -c
  <script>`, use the script's single `sys.argv = [...]` literal
  (ast.literal_eval, never executed). Zero or several literals, or
  non-string items, keep the OS argv (FOREIGN). The existing rules still
  require the random owner nonce and the ownership-scoped token path.

Tests: remote-lifecycle-launcher-ownership.test.ts (real local launcher
process: recognized; wrong nonce foreign; two literals foreign). The first
is red on base. Verified live against the mini's backend: real nonce
OWNED, wrong nonce FOREIGN.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 941478b69d979ac76395b8ab3895acd6dd02b378)
